WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:02.902
-Our next guest is a very
talented actress and comedian

00:00:02.902 --> 00:00:05.405
you know from shows like
"After Life" and "Motherland."

00:00:05.405 --> 00:00:07.573
Her very funny show
"Cunk on Earth"

00:00:07.573 --> 00:00:10.442
is streaming now on Netflix.
Let's take a look.

00:00:10.442 --> 00:00:13.112
-Just look at this 14th-century
painting of Jesus

00:00:13.112 --> 00:00:15.248
having his fateful tea party.

00:00:15.248 --> 00:00:17.349
The dimensions are all wrong.

00:00:17.349 --> 00:00:19.018
The room's squashed up

00:00:19.018 --> 00:00:22.188
so it looks like it's happening
in the lid of a cardboard box.

00:00:22.188 --> 00:00:25.124
Jesus is twice as big
as the others.

00:00:25.124 --> 00:00:27.127
So it looks like it's enjoying
an intimate dinner

00:00:27.127 --> 00:00:29.662
with some schoolboys --
not a good look.

00:00:29.662 --> 00:00:32.064
And what are those,
bowls or seashells?

00:00:32.064 --> 00:00:36.035
It's just shoddy craftsmanship.
Absolutely disgraceful.

00:00:36.035 --> 00:00:37.770
Shouldn't even be in a museum.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:40.339
But da Vinci's reboot
of the same painting

00:00:40.339 --> 00:00:41.740
is a different story.

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:44.176
He knew how to perspective
the [Bleep] out of things.

00:00:44.176 --> 00:00:46.779
Look at the angles of the walls
in "The Last Supper"

00:00:46.779 --> 00:00:49.282
and the table there.
You almost feel like

00:00:49.282 --> 00:00:51.985
you could crawl inside it
and betray Jesus yourself.

00:00:51.985 --> 00:00:53.286
[ Laughter ]

00:00:53.286 --> 00:00:55.755
-Please welcome to the show
Diane Morgan, everybody.

00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:58.457
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:58.457 --> 00:01:01.561
[ Mid-tempo music plays ]

00:01:01.561 --> 00:01:12.639
♪♪

00:01:12.639 --> 00:01:14.340
Welcome to the show, Diane.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:16.308
-Well, thanks for having me.

00:01:16.308 --> 00:01:18.144
-I'm really thrilled to
have you.

00:01:18.144 --> 00:01:20.547
I keep saying everybody
has been talking about "Cunk"

00:01:20.547 --> 00:01:23.115
for the last month to me.
And I love --

00:01:23.115 --> 00:01:24.451
I mean, the name Philomena Cunk

00:01:24.451 --> 00:01:26.085
is such a wonderful
character name.

00:01:26.085 --> 00:01:27.487
-Yeah.
-When you first saw it,

00:01:27.487 --> 00:01:29.589
did you know,
"Oh, that's really good"?

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:31.624
-Uh, yeah.
I thought, "That's weird."

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:34.494
-Yeah.
-And, well, it turns out

00:01:34.494 --> 00:01:36.863
that they wanted,
like, a posh character.

00:01:36.863 --> 00:01:39.332
And I'm not posh.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:43.602
And so, I went into the audition
for Philomena,

00:01:43.602 --> 00:01:47.006
did my best posh.
And but I knew

00:01:47.006 --> 00:01:49.208
that it would be just better
in my own accent,

00:01:49.208 --> 00:01:51.477
because it's so flat.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:51.477 --> 00:01:54.079
And depressing.
It just adds something.

00:01:54.079 --> 00:01:56.849
-Well, it is -- The most
wonderful thing is,

00:01:56.849 --> 00:01:59.218
you have this character who
is telling us about history

00:01:59.218 --> 00:02:03.256
and just seems not only
disinterested, deeply bored

00:02:03.256 --> 00:02:04.891
by everything.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:04.891 --> 00:02:07.126
And I think that's where the
flatness really comes in handy.

00:02:07.126 --> 00:02:08.560
-Absolutely.
'Cause I know

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:11.030
that everyone watching it
is bored by history, as well.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:14.167
[ Laughter ]
So I know what people want.

00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:16.202
-Yeah.
-I know.

00:02:16.202 --> 00:02:18.938
I know, when someone is being
really boring and dull,

00:02:18.938 --> 00:02:20.940
to move it on.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:23.242
Or just yawn in their face.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:23.242 --> 00:02:25.244
-It's true because when
you watch those history shows

00:02:25.244 --> 00:02:26.812
where the host is
really excited about it,

00:02:26.812 --> 00:02:28.948
it makes you feel bad
because you don't share it.

00:02:28.948 --> 00:02:30.883
-Yeah, 'cause you're thinking,
"This is boring."

00:02:30.883 --> 00:02:33.386
-Do you have any personal
interest in history yourself?

00:02:33.386 --> 00:02:35.388
-No, I hate it.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:39.058
Boring. Unless it's
something really weird.

00:02:39.058 --> 00:02:41.260
-Okay. So you --
-I like weird stuff.

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:42.861
-Yeah.
-I like to hear

00:02:42.861 --> 00:02:45.164
about the Bermuda Triangle,
stuff like that.

00:02:45.164 --> 00:02:46.666
-Okay, gotcha. So, that's --

00:02:46.666 --> 00:02:49.168
-I realize that's not history.
But, you know...

00:02:49.168 --> 00:02:52.271
I like -- Just saying
I like weird stuff.

00:02:52.271 --> 00:02:54.340
If it's not weird,
I'm not interested.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:57.977
-Yeah. So, you -- I know you've
done a couple series of this.

00:02:57.977 --> 00:03:01.013
You did "Cunk in Britain,"
and this is "Cunk on Earth."

00:03:01.013 --> 00:03:03.082
One of the things
that's been a through line is

00:03:03.082 --> 00:03:05.585
you do talk
to very informed people,

00:03:05.585 --> 00:03:08.420
experts, professors.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:12.225
And I mean, it's so wonderful
to watch you talk to them,

00:03:12.225 --> 00:03:14.560
because you ask them --
let's be honest --

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:16.496
very funny,
but very dumb questions.

00:03:16.496 --> 00:03:17.830
[ Laughter ]

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:20.299
-Yeah.
Very deep questions sometimes.

00:03:20.299 --> 00:03:21.634
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:21.634 --> 00:03:23.136
One of my favorites
in the series,

00:03:23.136 --> 00:03:28.073
you believe the Renaissance,
Cunk believes was --

00:03:28.073 --> 00:03:30.677
thought was a sauce.
-A nice sauce, yeah.

00:03:30.677 --> 00:03:32.678
[ Laughter ]

00:03:32.678 --> 00:03:35.381
-Now, while you're speaking
to this Renaissance expert,

00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:36.849
how much do they know
ahead of time,

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:37.883
when you shoot those,

00:03:37.883 --> 00:03:39.419
what they're
about to embark upon?

00:03:39.419 --> 00:03:43.055
-They don't know -- Well, they
know that it's a comedy now.

00:03:43.055 --> 00:03:44.924
The cat is out of the bag now.
-Right.

00:03:44.924 --> 00:03:46.725
-But they play along.
But they don't know

00:03:46.725 --> 00:03:48.094
what I'm going to ask.
-Yep.

00:03:48.094 --> 00:03:50.430
-So they don't know
any of the questions.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:53.432
And it's nice to sort of
back people into a corner...

00:03:53.432 --> 00:03:54.967
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:54.967 --> 00:03:57.936
-...and watch them struggle.
-They do a very --

00:03:57.936 --> 00:04:02.308
They do a very nice job
of not breaking, because --

00:04:02.308 --> 00:04:03.976
-They break.
-Yeah, and then you --

00:04:03.976 --> 00:04:05.811
-They just cut it out.
-You just edit it out, yeah.

00:04:05.811 --> 00:04:07.880
[ Laughter ]
Now, do you like them?

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:10.249
So, you, obviously, know
it's unusable once they break.

00:04:10.249 --> 00:04:12.351
But do you, as a performer,
enjoy the moment

00:04:12.351 --> 00:04:14.653
that you make these professors
and art historians laugh?

00:04:14.653 --> 00:04:17.824
-Yeah, but you think "Oh, no,
we'll have to go back

00:04:17.824 --> 00:04:19.992
and do it again."
[ Laughter ]

00:04:19.992 --> 00:04:23.062
-You do a wonderful job.

00:04:23.062 --> 00:04:25.932
It feels as though, you are --
everybody who makes this show

00:04:25.932 --> 00:04:27.666
is experts in the sort of tropes

00:04:27.666 --> 00:04:30.870
of these historical reenactment
or travel shows.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:33.806
You do a great amount
of walking in the woods.

00:04:33.806 --> 00:04:35.408
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:35.408 --> 00:04:38.577
-A lot of walking and talking.
And we were talking backstage,

00:04:38.577 --> 00:04:40.813
there's a scene
in the second episode

00:04:40.813 --> 00:04:43.382
where, I mean, I think it's
about a four-minute monolog

00:04:43.382 --> 00:04:44.984
where you're walking
around a castle.

00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:46.485
-Yeah.
-I'm so impressed

00:04:46.485 --> 00:04:47.920
by the amount of
walking and talking you do.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:49.288
-I do a lot of walking.
-Yeah.

00:04:49.288 --> 00:04:50.489
Do you enjoy being
out in the woods,

00:04:50.489 --> 00:04:52.958
doing your walking and talking?
-Uh, not really.

00:04:52.958 --> 00:04:54.326
It's very cold.
-Yeah.

00:04:54.326 --> 00:04:56.862
-But it's just
one of those tropes

00:04:56.862 --> 00:04:58.997
that history documentaries
always have.

00:04:58.997 --> 00:05:00.733
They're always walking
through the woods.

00:05:00.733 --> 00:05:02.101
[ Laughter ]

00:05:02.101 --> 00:05:04.670
Or just staring off
into the middle distance.

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:06.405
[ Laughter ]
You know?

00:05:06.405 --> 00:05:09.742
-You do wonderful
as a performer.

00:05:09.742 --> 00:05:12.711
I'm very taken
with how Cunk trips a lot.

00:05:12.711 --> 00:05:16.515
-I love a trip.
I love a bit of physical comedy.

00:05:16.515 --> 00:05:22.021
-They're all very little trips
that you try to then play off.

00:05:22.021 --> 00:05:23.189
-Yeah.

00:05:23.189 --> 00:05:24.790
-I'm such a fan
of the little trip.

00:05:24.790 --> 00:05:27.293
-Are you? I love a trip!
-Oh, I love a trip.

00:05:27.293 --> 00:05:31.130
Especially from someone
who is hosting a history show

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:32.765
and there's
this real gravitas to it,

00:05:32.765 --> 00:05:34.600
and then a little trip.
-Fall over. Yeah.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:36.035
-Yeah.
-Do you know

00:05:36.035 --> 00:05:37.470
when you've done a good one
when you're shooting?

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:39.237
You're like, "Perfect, yeah"?
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:39.237 --> 00:05:41.774
I mean, there was one I rolled
down a hill at one point.

00:05:41.774 --> 00:05:43.275
[ Laughter ]

00:05:43.275 --> 00:05:45.645
Which I was at the top
of a sand dune.

00:05:45.645 --> 00:05:48.314
And I thought -- I didn't tell
anyone I was going to do it.

00:05:48.314 --> 00:05:50.750
I just thought, "This is
going to be quite boring,

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:54.153
me walking down a sand dune.
So, I'm just gonna fall over."

00:05:54.153 --> 00:05:55.488
[ Laughter ]

00:05:55.488 --> 00:05:58.056
No one came to help me.
I was rolling.

00:05:58.056 --> 00:06:00.426
[ Laughter ]
Just kept rolling.

00:06:00.426 --> 00:06:02.361
And they were just filming.

00:06:02.361 --> 00:06:04.563
[ Laughter ]

00:06:04.563 --> 00:06:06.666
-This is how much you care
about your craft,

00:06:06.666 --> 00:06:08.534
you're willing to take a header.
-I'm just worried

00:06:08.534 --> 00:06:11.336
that people are gonna be bored,
so I think, "I'll fall over."

00:06:11.336 --> 00:06:13.005
[ Laughter ]
-Now, again,

00:06:13.005 --> 00:06:15.207
because there's
such attention to detail,

00:06:15.207 --> 00:06:17.677
have people, when this
first started in the U.K.,

00:06:17.677 --> 00:06:20.046
did people think
it was a real show?

00:06:20.046 --> 00:06:21.381
-Yes.
-Okay, so, they did.

00:06:21.381 --> 00:06:22.982
-And they still do.
-They still do?

00:06:22.982 --> 00:06:25.017
[ Laughter ]
-On Twitter, I still get people

00:06:25.017 --> 00:06:27.720
going, "This presenter
is terrible."

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:30.156
[ Laughter ]

00:06:30.156 --> 00:06:32.758
"She doesn't know
what she's talking about.

00:06:32.758 --> 00:06:34.527
She's getting
all these facts wrong."

00:06:34.527 --> 00:06:36.462
[ Laughter ]

00:06:36.462 --> 00:06:38.698
"The BBC
are really dumbing down."

00:06:38.698 --> 00:06:41.734
[ Laughter ]
-Now, were you --

00:06:41.734 --> 00:06:44.137
Did you originally think
comedy was your path?

00:06:44.137 --> 00:06:46.572
Or did you think
just acting in general?

00:06:46.572 --> 00:06:50.576
-No. I wanted to be
a comedy actor from age 15.

00:06:50.576 --> 00:06:52.712
-Oh, wonderful.
-That's what I wanted to do.

00:06:52.712 --> 00:06:55.847
You know, I grew up watching,
like, Harold Lloyd

00:06:55.847 --> 00:06:58.751
and Laurel &amp; Hardy, and
I was just, you know,

00:06:58.751 --> 00:07:01.020
obsessed
that's what I wanted to do.

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:02.955
-And did you do it at all
at university?

00:07:02.955 --> 00:07:04.624
Or did you --
-I went to drama school.

00:07:04.624 --> 00:07:05.891
-Okay, gotcha.
-Yeah.

00:07:05.891 --> 00:07:07.893
-And then how were they
with attempting comedy?

00:07:07.893 --> 00:07:09.528
Were they alright with it?
-Well, I went --

00:07:09.528 --> 00:07:13.065
I went to the head of the school
and I said,

00:07:13.065 --> 00:07:16.135
"Look, I'm not interested
in Shakespeare, you know?

00:07:16.135 --> 00:07:19.571
I only want to do comedy.
So just let me do --

00:07:19.571 --> 00:07:21.840
I just want to get better
at comedy."

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:26.479
And he started giving me, like,
Lady Macbeth, parts like that.

00:07:26.479 --> 00:07:30.349
But, luckily, I got big laughs
as lady Macbeth.

00:07:30.349 --> 00:07:31.884
[ Laughter ]
I was really proud.

00:07:31.884 --> 00:07:33.752
-To this day, they say you were
the funniest Lady Macbeth.

00:07:33.752 --> 00:07:35.754
-The funniest Lady Macbeth,
yeah.

00:07:35.754 --> 00:07:38.356
-And then, it is true that you
did stand-up for a while?

00:07:38.356 --> 00:07:40.826
-I did it for nearly 10 years.
-And did you love it?

00:07:40.826 --> 00:07:43.028
-I hated it.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:43.028 --> 00:07:46.399
I hated it.
Did you do stand-up?

00:07:46.399 --> 00:07:47.666
-I do, still, a little.
-Did you hate --

00:07:47.666 --> 00:07:49.201
You still do it?
-I do, yeah.

00:07:49.201 --> 00:07:51.236
-Do you hate it?
-I don't hate it, no.

00:07:51.236 --> 00:07:53.706
-Why not? It's awful.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:53.706 --> 00:07:57.476
-What did you hate about it?
-just going about at night

00:07:57.476 --> 00:08:00.079
on your own,
traveling around late at night.

00:08:00.079 --> 00:08:03.916
Dealing with crowds
that, you know, were horrible.

00:08:03.916 --> 00:08:05.551
[ Laughter ]
You could just tell,

00:08:05.551 --> 00:08:07.486
some nights,
they were just horrible people.

00:08:07.486 --> 00:08:10.222
And be like, "Ugh."
You'd have to pander to them.

00:08:10.222 --> 00:08:12.892
-Yeah.
-I just-- I just felt

00:08:12.892 --> 00:08:15.194
physically sick
every time I did it.

00:08:15.194 --> 00:08:16.662
[ Laughter ]
-Do you feel great relief

00:08:16.662 --> 00:08:18.631
being past
that part of your career?

00:08:18.631 --> 00:08:21.801
-Yes, absolutely, I mean,
you know, fingers crossed.

00:08:21.801 --> 00:08:23.669
Knock on wood.
-Well, I certainly --

00:08:23.669 --> 00:08:25.370
-I might have to go back
at some point.

00:08:25.370 --> 00:08:27.273
-Base on what you've said,
I won't be very excited

00:08:27.273 --> 00:08:28.941
if I'm in an audience
and you walk out.

00:08:28.941 --> 00:08:30.976
[ Laughter ]

00:08:30.976 --> 00:08:33.246
-Oh, no.
-And now, we were talking

00:08:33.246 --> 00:08:34.980
about this backstage,
but, you know,

00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:36.882
this hasn't been on Netflix
very long,

00:08:36.882 --> 00:08:38.150
but here you are
in New York City,

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:40.853
and people are recognizing
you from "Cunk"?

00:08:40.853 --> 00:08:42.788
-It's amazing.
People are lovely.

00:08:42.788 --> 00:08:46.191
They're so enthusiastic.
-Well, it is a show

00:08:46.191 --> 00:08:47.926
that deserves
all the enthusiasm.

00:08:47.926 --> 00:08:49.361
It's really wonderful.
And thank you so much

00:08:49.361 --> 00:08:51.129
for being here.
-Thank you for having me.

00:08:51.129 --> 00:08:53.032
-I'm just delighted
that you're here.

00:08:53.032 --> 00:08:54.900
[ Cheers and applause ]
You guys, that's Diane Morgan.

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:56.735
"Cunk on Earth"
is streaming now on Netflix.

00:08:56.735 --> 00:08:58.404
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

